+## 3. Source investigation of OPEN[MOH-PROGRAM]
+
+### 3.1 Proposition 5.3 and the bottom polynomial
+
+#### Local degrees and what a selected path forgets
+
+At a node $D_j$ of a tower, Props.4.6 and 5.3 (p.170(4)--(5),
+pp.180--183) give
+
+\[
+ P_j=\deg p_j=V_{j+1}{d_j\over d_{j+1}},\qquad
+ Q_j=\deg q_j=V_{j+1}{n-M_j\over d_{j+1}},             \tag{3.1}
+\]
+
+where $q_j$ is squarefree, every root of $p_j$ is a root of $q_j$,
+$p_j$ is not a power of $q_j$, and at least one $p_j$-root has
+multiplicity
+
+\[
+ u>{P_j\over Q_j}={d_j\over n-M_j}.                    \tag{3.2}
+\]
+
+Proposition 5.3 does not merely say that one may select one such $u$.  Its
+proof applies to any factor above the threshold, and the summary theorem on
+p.200(4) says every subdisc with more than $n/(n-M_j)$ roots of $g$ extends
+the tower.  Consequently all major siblings must be followed.  A row
+containing only one chosen sequence $(V_s,\ldots,V_2)$ forgets this universal
+quantifier.
+
+The p.201 conjugation makes this finite.  With $A=A_j$ and
+$P=P_j$, an actual factorization has the form
+
+\[
+ P=b+A\sum_{\ell=1}^{t}u_\ell,                         \tag{3.3}
+\]
+
+where $b\ge0$ is the multiplicity at the fixed root $0$, and each $u_\ell$
+is the per-root multiplicity of a nonzero $A$-orbit.  Thus
+$b\equiv P\pmod A$.  Lemma 5.2 also gives
+
+\[
+ A\mid Q-1,                                             \tag{3.4}
+\]
+
+so $q$ has one fixed root and $(Q-1)/A$ nonzero root orbits; unused slots
+are $q$-roots with $p$-multiplicity zero.  Equation (3.4) was asserted and
+checked for every baseline row; it is automatic and by itself kills none.
+
+The frozen driver tests (10)/(11) only for the selected $V_j$.  The complete
+necessary test instead enumerates every (3.3), embeds the selected $V_j$ as
+either $b$ or an $u_\ell$, and recursively checks every $b$ or $u_\ell$
+satisfying (3.2).  Minor factors stop, exactly as p.200(5) says.  At $j=2$
+every major child must satisfy (12) or (13).
+
+#### A source theorem recovered from the construction
+
+**WHOLE-MAJOR-TREE NECESSITY.**  If a nonreducible constant-Jacobian pair
+realizes a path row, then at every node there exists an orbit partition
+(3.3), within the $q$-root capacity (3.4), such that every factor above
+(3.2) admits a complete lower major subtree, every bottom major leaf obeys
+(12)/(13), and the recorded $V$-path is one path in that tree.
+
+**SOURCE-DERIVED NECESSARY:** the implication is assembled from
+Prop.4.6(1)--(5), pp.170--171; Prop.5.3, pp.180--183; Def.5.1, p.179; the
+p.201 orbit action; and summary Theorem (4)--(7), p.200.  It is uniform in
+$D$.  Moh does not state the assembled dynamic-program theorem, it is not a
+sufficiency theorem for constructing $f,g$, and no source says that the CDC
+program implemented this exact state space.
+
+The two independent implementations agree on all 658 input rows.  The bare
+source theorem leaves 60 rows / 40 groups / 12 $(n,m)$ classes, including all
+six printed rows, and it already produces the exact $(75,50)$ discriminator.
+Thus it accounts for 598 of the 652 excess rows.  It also explains why the
+charged numerical `NOT-ALL-(11)` is too weak: it checks one possible path;
+WHOLE-MAJOR-TREE checks all forced sibling paths.
+
+#### The differential equation adds a real sibling condition
+
+Appendix I Prop.A.3 (p.205) is a summary of the equation
+
+\[
+ D(P,Q,p,q)=Ppq'-Qqp'=cp,qquad c\ne0.                  \tag{3.5}
+\]
+
+At a simple $q$-root $a$ with $u=\operatorname{mult}_a p$, division by the
+local power of $\pi-a$ gives
+
+\[
+ (P-Qu)q_a(a)=c,qquad q_a=q/(\pi-a).                  \tag{3.6}
+\]
+
+Therefore
+
+\[
+ \boxed{P-Qu\ne0\quad\hbox{for every }p\hbox{-root}.}  \tag{3.7}
+\]
+
+For the selected major root (3.7) follows already from strict (3.2), but it
+can exclude a *minor sibling* of multiplicity exactly $P/Q$.  This is an
+exact source consequence, not a guessed no-common-root rule.
+
+For completeness I also implemented a stronger, externally motivated
+passport necessity.  Put $z=\pi^A$, pad the nonzero-orbit multiplicities by
+zeros to $S=(Q-1)/A$ slots, and define
+
+\[
+ W_0={P-Qb\over A},\qquad W_\ell=P-Qu_\ell.
+\]
+
+The logarithmic derivative of $p^Q/q^P$ shows that all $W$ are nonzero and,
+after $g=\gcd(|W_i|)$ and
+$d_+=\sum_{W_i>0}W_i$, a necessary reduced-passport inequality is
+
+\[
+ d_+/g\ge S.                                            \tag{3.8}
+\]
+
+This is the polynomial-abc/Davenport--Zannier degree obstruction for the
+cyclic quotient; it is not stated by Moh and is kept typed as an external
+candidate.  It leaves the same 20-row set at $n\le100$ as the local tests
+when combined with the exploratory recenter rule below.  Hence even the
+stronger root-position probe does not supply the missing exact discriminator.
+
+### 3.2 Proposition 5.5 and the r=2 alternatives
+
+**No omitted second numerical clause was found.**  The proof on pp.186--188
+first treats the special case $s=2$.  Proposition 5.4 then forces
+$M_2=n-2$, $\delta_2=-1$, and $d_2>V_2>d_2/2$.  The coprimality and conjugacy
+argument yields (12)/(13), exactly as re-derived in §1.3.
+
+After the two residue alternatives Moh sets, in one branch,
+$n^*V_2=AD$ and $m^*V_2-1=AE$ and obtains
+
+\[
+ V_2>V_2-U_2=C(D+E)\ge D\ge V_2,
+\]
+
+with the other branch symmetric.  That contradiction uses the special
+$s=2$ fraction for $\delta_1$ and $U_2=d_2-V_2$.  For $s\ge3$ the higher
+product factors in Def.5.1(3) are present.  Reusing this inequality as a
+general bottom filter would therefore be an invalid transport of a
+special-case proof.  The implementation tried no such promotion.
+
+### 3.3 Proposition 5.6 and branch data
+
+Proposition 5.6 (statement p.188 bottom, proof p.189) says that if the bottom
+general point is literally
+
+\[
+\sigma_1=\pi t^{\delta_1},
+\]
+
+then the rings already agree or a polynomial automorphism simultaneously
+reduces the relevant degrees.  Both outcomes contradict the p.200 search
+premise.  The operative datum is therefore a typed edge label:
+
+```text
+Z_j : the actual selected factor is pi       (zero fixed point);
+N_j : the actual selected factor is pi-a     (a != 0).
+```
+
+The implications are only
+
+\[
+N_j\Rightarrow V_j\le\triangle_j,
+\qquad
+Z_j\Rightarrow V_j\equiv\square_j\pmod {A_j}.          \tag{3.9}
+\]
+
+The converses are false.  In particular the printed row
+$(84,56;M_2=72,V_2=5)$ satisfies both numerical alternatives.  It cannot be
+declared a zero branch from its congruence.  The exact path statement is
+“not all *typed* edges are $Z$,” including the $j=2$ edge.  There is no extra
+$A_1$ edge: $A_1$ controls the bottom degree residues, not a $V_1$ choice.
+
+The safe branch-free projection used here is stronger and universal.  If
+the mandatory residue $b=P\bmod A$ is positive and above the major
+threshold, an actual zero major sibling exists regardless of which factor
+the recorded path selected.  Follow that forced zero sibling recursively;
+if it reaches the bottom still major, Prop.5.6 rejects the skeleton.  This
+`C_ZERO_PATH` condition leaves 153/100/15 rows/groups/classes and has exactly
+the desired action at $(75,50)$.  WHOLE-MAJOR-TREE is its full sibling-aware
+version.
+
+I also tested an **inferred, not promoted** recenter state: a nonzero edge at
+an integral radius $\delta\le0$ was treated as removable by a polynomial
+translation and hence as not breaking the pure path.  The motivation is the
+explicit $y\mapsto y-ax-b$ reduction on p.190 and the polynomial-center work
+inside Prop.5.4.  But Prop.5.4 proves this only at its globally selected
+$i=\max\{r:P_r>V_r\}$; it does not state the edgewise rule for every sibling.
+This exploratory strengthening reduces 60 to 23, and with (3.7) to 20, but
+it is **not PROVED-IN-SOURCE** as a universal tree rule.
+
+### 3.4 Section 6 minor-disc restrictions
+
+The minor-disc read produced two clean scalar necessities, both already
+automatic on the 658 rows:
+
+* Lemma 5.3 (pp.185--186) and the two-factor highest form used on p.194 give
+  $d_s>V_s>d_s/2$.  The frozen generic (7) writes a weak upper bound, but all
+  enumerated rows already have the strict one.
+* On p.194 Moh computes
+  \[
+  \delta_{s-1}={u_s(n-M_{s-1})-d_s\over
+                     V_s(n-M_{s-1})-d_s}\ge0,
+  \qquad u_s=d_s-V_s.
+  \]
+  Equivalently the numerator is nonnegative.  Again all 658 pass.
+
+Proposition 6.1 (pp.190--193) says a minor sibling has next radius at least
+one and, under the negative-order hypothesis, is a distribution detector.
+The summary on p.200 restates the major/minor threshold and bounds the number
+of subdiscs by $Q_j$.  The former is (7); the latter is exactly the root
+capacity used in (3.3)--(3.4).  Props.6.2--6.4 (pp.195--199) give bidegrees
+and conditional transformations, not another predicate on $(M,V)$.  In
+particular $u_s=1$ is sufficient for Prop.6.4, not necessary; imposing it
+would kill Moh's $(99,66)$ class, where $(d_s,V_s,u_s)=(11,8,3)$.
+
+Cor.6.1 (pp.199--200) excludes $d_s=3$ for a least-degree counterexample.
+That is already printed as $d_s\ge4$ in (6).  No general condition on a
+second point at infinity, an $m$-divisibility, or a further sign/order of
+the $M_i$ was found in this section.
+
+### 3.5 Appendix II eliminations
+
+Appendix II cannot be the pre-table eliminator: it begins on p.207 by taking
+the p.202 table as its input.  For the first three degree classes, where
+$u_3=d_3-V_3=1$, Props.6.4 and 6.3 transform the data to
+
+```text
+(16,12; M2=13,V2=3),
+(21,14; M2=16[18],V2=2[5]),
+(15,10; M2=11,V2=3[2]).
+```
+
+This carries all five expanded rows forward; it deletes none.  Pages
+207--209 use quasi-approximate roots, Tschirnhausen changes, and degree/order
+bounds to lower coefficient counts.  The only fully displayed contradiction
+is pp.210--211 for transformed $(15,10,M_2=11,V_2=3)$.  There Moh obtains the
+case-specific $f$-root split $2,2,6$, bounds a remainder $\gamma$, and splits
+on a coefficient $a_9=0$ or $a_9\ne0$.  The bracketed $V_2=2$ row and the
+other degree classes are covered only by the closing assertion that similar
+direct computations work.
+
+For $(99,66)$, pp.209--210 split according to whether a leading polynomial
+is a power of a linear polynomial or a ninth power of a two-root cubic and
+reduce the coefficient count.  Again no displayed general contradiction or
+pre-table integer rule results.
+
+In particular Appendix II contains no uniform filter on $H=L_1^uL_2^v$, no
+new $m\mid(\cdots)$ condition, no second-infinity indicator, no all-level
+ordering of $M_i$, and no pre-table root-count predicate.  Its $2,2,6$ split
+is realization data for one transformed row, not a theorem for every node.
+The appendix's role is case-by-case and post-census.
+
+### 3.6 Abhyankar--Moh semigroup candidates
+
+The correct generators are not the literal $M_i$.  From p.150, set
+
+\[
+d_1=n,\quad n_i={d_i\over d_{i+1}},\quad
+\mu_1=M_1=-m,\quad
+\mu_i=n_{i-1}\mu_{i-1}+M_i-M_{i-1},\quad R_i=-\mu_i.
+\]
+
+Proposition 2.2 and p.154 identify $R_i$ with the $y$-degree of the
+specialized approximate root.  Proposition 3.1, pp.157--159, gives the exact
+bounded-digit condition: for each effective $r$ there are unique admissible
+indices satisfying
+
+\[
+ jn+\sum_{i=1}^{r-1}R_i\alpha_i=n_rR_r,qquad
+ j\ge0,\quad0\le\alpha_i<n_i,\quad\alpha_r=0.           \tag{3.10}
+\]
+
+This is stronger than unbounded membership in
+$\langle n,R_1,\ldots,R_{r-1}\rangle$.  It is
+**PROVED-IN-SOURCE**, and printed condition (4)'s phrase “is ...
+characteristic data” logically imports it.  It is not implied by arbitrary
+ordered gcd data: Moh's warning on p.151 that degrees $8,6$ cannot have
+$M_2=13$ is an example.
+
+The correctly indexed finite implementation of (3.10), both through the
+effective terminal $M_s=n-2$ and with the endpoint omitted as a control,
+passes all 658 rows and all 23,720 rows at $48\le D\le200$.  It therefore
+kills none of the 54 bare-tree, 17 recenter-tree, or 14 ODE residual rows.
+The usual strict approximate-root inequality is likewise automatic.
+Alternative shifted indices or reversed inequalities kill four to six
+printed rows and fail closed.
+
+No conductor equality, symmetry condition, or assertion that
+$\{n,M_1,\ldots,M_s\}$ itself generates the value semigroup appears on the
+audited pages.  Such a rule would also use the wrong generator map.  Thus
+item (vi) is excluded as a discriminator at this bound to the extent of
+Prop.3.1 and the standard characteristic-sequence inequalities; a stronger
+cross-polynomial realization theorem remains a ranked residual, not a
+source finding.
+
