+### 0.2 Image-first protocol and a necessary type distinction
+
+I rendered the source at 300 dpi with `pdftoppm`, using journal page $p$ at
+PDF page $p-139$.  I read pp.179--189, p.194, pp.198--202, and Appendix II
+from the PNGs.  I also read pp.140--159, pp.163--178, and Appendix I where a
+definition or differential equation was needed.  Appendix I begins in the
+lower half of p.202 and ends at p.207; Appendix II is pp.207--211; p.212 is
+the references.  Formulae were never recovered from `pdftotext`.
+
+There is a type issue in the charged headline that affects claims (3), (5),
+and (6).  Printed conditions (3) and (4) are not predicates on an integer
+tuple: they assert a constant-Jacobian, degree-irreducible polynomial pair and
+that the displayed integers really are part of its characteristic data.
+The driver can enforce their stated *arithmetic consequences*, but cannot
+certify either assertion.  I therefore use
+
+```text
+PATH-ARITH(1--13)
+```
+
+for the finite space generated by the frozen driver.  Calling its 658 rows
+“polynomial pairs satisfying (1)--(13)” would convert a necessary-condition
+screen into an attainment theorem.  This repair does not change any count.
+
+### 0.3 Executive finding
+
+The numerical reproductions and the p.202 erratum survive review.  The main
+charge does not close in the requested exact form: neither Moh's text nor the
+available artifact contains a source-proved discriminator whose survivor set
+is exactly the six printed rows.  I did, however, recover a large omitted
+*global* necessary condition: the selected $V$-sequence must embed in the
+complete orbit-factor tree, and **every** above-threshold factor must
+recursively extend.  The source-derived tree plus the local ODE/passport
+necessities leaves 55 rows (six plus 49), down from 658.  An additional
+edgewise polynomial-recentring rule, useful but not proved by Prop.5.4 for
+arbitrary siblings, narrows this experimentally to
+
+```text
+658 PATH-ARITH rows -> 20 rows = six printed + 14 excess
+508 groups          -> 12 groups = five printed groups + seven excess
+63 (n,m) classes    -> 7 classes = four printed classes + three excess.
+```
+
+Both the source-derived and exploratory closures have, at $(75,50)$, exactly
+the requested action: they
+delete $M_2\in\{5,10,40,60\}$ and retain
+$(M_2,M_3)=(55,73)$ with $V_2\in\{2,3\}$.  It is uniform in degree, not an
+Appendix-II case split.  But because 14 excess rows remain, it is a partial
+answer, not `OPEN[MOH-PROGRAM]`'s demanded discriminator.
+
+## 1. Independent source transcription and notation
+
+### 1.1 Conditions (8)--(13), transcribed from page images
+
+The following was transcribed directly from the 300-dpi rendering of journal
+p.201 (PDF p.62), before either charged report was opened.  Bars and subscripts
+are retained because they matter to the two factor cases.
+
+> **(8)** the radii $\delta_s,\ldots,\delta_1$ of
+> $D_s,\ldots,D_1$ can be computed from Definition 5.1.  We shall consider
+> the increment of the denominator $A_{r-1}$ of $\delta_{r-1}$ with respect
+> to $\delta_s,\ldots,\delta_r$.  Namely let the l.c.m. of the reduced
+> denominators of $\delta_s,\ldots,\delta_r$ be $L$.  Then $A_{r-1}$ is
+> defined to be the reduced denominator of $L\delta_{r-1}$.  Let us consider
+> the following division algorithm equation
+>
+> **(9)** $\displaystyle V_r\left({d_{r-1}\over d_r}\right)
+> =\triangle_{r-1}A_{r-1}+\square_{r-1}$.
+>
+> Let $(\bar t)^{LA_{r-1}}=t$.  Due to the existence of the following
+> automorphism of $k\langle\!\langle\bar t\rangle\!\rangle$ over
+> $k\langle\!\langle\bar t^{A_{r-1}}\rangle\!\rangle$,
+> $\bar t\mapsto\omega\bar t$, where $\omega$ is a $A_{r-1}$-th root of
+> unity, the value of $V_{r-1}$ is further restricted by
+>
+> **(10)** $\displaystyle V_{r-1}\leq\triangle_{r-1}$
+>
+> if the corresponding factor of $p(\bar\pi)$ is of the form
+> $\bar\pi-a$ with $a\ne0$ or for some $j$ the following
+>
+> **(11)** $\displaystyle V_{r-1}=jA_{r-1}+\square_{r-1}$
+>
+> if the corresponding factor of $p(\bar\pi)$ is of the form $\bar\pi$.
+> Finally, when $r=2$ we must have the following (cf. the proof of
+> Proposition 5.5):
+>
+> **(12)** $\displaystyle A_1\mid\left({n\over d_2}\right)V_2,\qquad
+> A_1\mid\left({m\over d_2}\right)V_2-1$
+>
+> or
+>
+> **(13)** $\displaystyle A_1\mid\left({m\over d_2}\right)V_2,\qquad
+> A_1\mid\left({n\over d_2}\right)V_2-1$.
+
+Immediately after (13), Moh adds: “the situation indicated by the equation
+(11) can not always happen as established by Proposition 5.6.”  That sentence
+is not itself a quantified search condition and will be audited against
+Proposition 5.6 below.
+
+### 1.2 Diff against the charged transcription
+
+Only after fixing the preceding transcription did I open charged §1.2.  The
+mathematical diff is empty:
+
+* the charged $L$, $A_{r-1}$, quotient $\triangle_{r-1}$, and remainder
+  $\square_{r-1}$ have the same definitions as the image;
+* its (9), (10), (11), (12), and (13) have the same indices, inequality,
+  congruence branch, and two bottom conjunctions;
+* its fixed field is correctly written
+  $k\langle\!\langle\bar t^{A_{r-1}}\rangle\!\rangle$.
+
+There are only transcription conventions: charged §1.2 renders Moh's
+old-style $\preccurlyeq$ glyph as `<=`, expands the division-algorithm fact
+$0\leq\square<A$, and uses ASCII `|` for divisibility.  Those are faithful,
+not substantive alterations.  The independent read also confirms that
+$(8)$--$(11)$ constrain $V_{r-1}$ for $r=s,\ldots,3$; (12)/(13) are the
+separate $r=2$ endpoint.  There is no $V_1$.
+
+One terminology repair is useful.  The polynomial $p(\pi)$ is the common
+leading polynomial supplied by Prop.4.6 at the general point of the current
+major disc (p.170(1)--(5), used on pp.182--183).  Calling it “the bottom
+polynomial” is harmless locally, but it is a new polynomial at every node of
+the factor tree, not one global polynomial reused through the tower.
+
+The p.202 columns $M_4=63,83$ in the first two classes are also not missing
+search data.  They are the successor characteristic value $n-1$, needed when
+the gcd after $M_s=n-2$ is still greater than one (pp.150--151).  The searched
+effective prefix is therefore $(52,62)$ or $(64/72,82)$, exactly as encoded by
+the driver.
+
+### 1.3 Re-derivation of the p.188 identity
+
+The derivation does not need the charged report.  In Prop.5.5 Moh writes on
+p.187, with
+
+\[
+n=d_2n^*,\qquad -M_1=d_2m^*,\qquad U_2=d_2-V_2,
+\]
+
+the reduced fraction
+
+\[
+ \delta_1=
+ { (n^*+m^*)U_2-1\over (n^*+m^*)V_2-1}={B\over A}.
+\]
+
+Because $B/A$ is reduced, its denominator divides the denominator before
+reduction:
+
+\[
+ \boxed{A\mid(n^*+m^*)V_2-1}.                         \tag{1.1}
+\]
+
+For a longer tower the same identity for Moh's relative denominator $A_1$
+comes from Lemma 5.2 (pp.177--179), rather than from pretending the $s=2$
+fraction is unchanged.  Put $b=(n-M_1)/d_2=n^*+m^*$ and
+
+\[
+R=d_2\delta_s+\sum_{i=3}^sV_i{d_2\over d_i}
+  (\delta_{i-1}-\delta_i)-V_2\delta_2.
+\]
+
+Every denominator in $R$ divides
+$L_1=\operatorname{lcm}(\operatorname{den}\delta_s,\ldots,
+\operatorname{den}\delta_2)$.  Lemma 5.2 at $M_1$ gives
+
+\[
+b(R+V_2\delta_1)=-1+\delta_1,
+\]
+
+hence
+
+\[
+(bV_2-1)(L_1\delta_1)=-L_1-b(L_1R)\in\mathbb Z.
+\]
+
+Since $A_1$ is the reduced denominator of $L_1\delta_1$, (1.1) follows.
+On p.188 Moh combines it with coprimality of
+$g_\sigma,T^\psi_{1,\sigma}$ and of their derivatives.  Exactly one of their
+degrees is $0\pmod {A_1}$ and the other is $1\pmod {A_1}$, yielding precisely
+(12) or (13).  No third residue or second bottom clause appears.
